From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: keep a port's bridge valid for taggers until
 they are done with it

A tagger reads dp->bridge on transmit, without locks, to handle a frame
marked for TX forwarding offload, while dsa_port_bridge_create() and
dsa_port_bridge_destroy() set and clear it under RTNL. Nothing orders
the publication of a new bridge after its initialisation, the
dsa_port_bridge_dev_get() and dsa_port_bridge_num_get() helpers load
dp->bridge twice, and the last port to leave a bridge releases its
number and frees it immediately. DSA clears BR_TX_FWD_OFFLOAD at
NETDEV_PRECHANGEUPPER, before it destroys the bridge at
NETDEV_CHANGEUPPER, but does not wait in between for frames already past
that test, and a marked frame can also wait in a qdisc installed on the
user port (DSA user ports have none by default). A tagger can then
dereference a NULL or freed bridge, or tag a frame with a bridge number
that a new bridge already reuses.

Publish dp->bridge with smp_store_release() and clear it with
WRITE_ONCE(), make the two helpers load it once, and when the last
reference goes, wait for an RCU grace period (synchronize_net(), as the
bridge does when a port leaves) before giving the number back and
freeing the bridge. The transmit path, including qdisc dequeue, runs
under rcu_read_lock_bh().

This keeps dp->bridge a plain pointer rather than converting it to
__rcu, which would touch every driver that reads it under RTNL.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 include/net/dsa.h |  8 ++++++--
 net/dsa/port.c    | 15 ++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

--- a/include/net/dsa.h
+++ b/include/net/dsa.h
@@ -766,12 +766,16 @@ struct net_device *dsa_port_to_bridge_port(const struct dsa_port *dp)
 static inline struct net_device *
 dsa_port_bridge_dev_get(const struct dsa_port *dp)
 {
-	return dp->bridge ? dp->bridge->dev : NULL;
+	const struct dsa_bridge *bridge = READ_ONCE(dp->bridge);
+
+	return bridge ? bridge->dev : NULL;
 }
 
 static inline unsigned int dsa_port_bridge_num_get(struct dsa_port *dp)
 {
-	return dp->bridge ? dp->bridge->num : 0;
+	const struct dsa_bridge *bridge = READ_ONCE(dp->bridge);
+
+	return bridge ? bridge->num : 0;
 }
 
 static inline bool dsa_port_bridge_same(const struct dsa_port *a,
--- a/net/dsa/port.c
+++ b/net/dsa/port.c
@@ -427,7 +427,8 @@ static int dsa_port_bridge_create(struct dsa_port *dp,
 	bridge = dsa_tree_bridge_find(ds->dst, br);
 	if (bridge) {
 		refcount_inc(&bridge->refcount);
-		dp->bridge = bridge;
+		/* Taggers read dp->bridge locklessly on transmit */
+		smp_store_release(&dp->bridge, bridge);
 		return 0;
 	}
 
@@ -447,7 +448,8 @@ static int dsa_port_bridge_create(struct dsa_port *dp,
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 	}
 
-	dp->bridge = bridge;
+	/* Taggers read dp->bridge locklessly on transmit */
+	smp_store_release(&dp->bridge, bridge);
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -457,11 +459,18 @@ static void dsa_port_bridge_destroy(struct dsa_port *dp,
 {
 	struct dsa_bridge *bridge = dp->bridge;
 
-	dp->bridge = NULL;
+	WRITE_ONCE(dp->bridge, NULL);
 
 	if (!refcount_dec_and_test(&bridge->refcount))
 		return;
 
+	/* A tagger may still be using this bridge for a frame marked for TX
+	 * forwarding offload, from the transmit path under RCU. Let it finish
+	 * before the bridge number can go to a new bridge and the memory is
+	 * freed.
+	 */
+	synchronize_net();
+
 	if (bridge->num)
 		dsa_bridge_num_put(br, bridge->num);
 
